//! The reference client's icon set, compiled into egui shapes.
//!
//! **Generated by `tools/gen_icons_egui.py`. Do not edit.**
//!
//! This is the egui twin of `crates/sift-desktop/src/icons_gen.rs`: the
//! same 441 SVGs out of `vendor/modrinth-app/assets/icons`, read by
//! the same parser, listed in the same order under the same names, so the two
//! files can be diffed a glyph at a time. The parser is not copied --
//! `tools/gen_icons_egui.py` imports `tools/gen_icons.py` and calls its
//! `read_icon` -- because two SVG readers in one tree is two answers to what
//! an icon looks like, and only one of them gets fixed.
//!
//! | table | where | variants | glyphs |
//! | --- | --- | --- | --- |
//! | `ALL` | `icons/` | `none` | 313 |
//! | `TAG_CATEGORIES` | `icons/tags/categories` | `TagCategory` | 102 |
//! | `TAG_LOADERS` | `icons/tags/loaders` | `TagLoader` | 26 |
//!
//! The prefixes are the reference's own, and the four tag icons the parser
//! refuses are absent here for the same reason they are absent there:
//!
//! * `tags/loaders/geyser.svg` -- non-uniform transform scale (1.0404 x 1.1631)
//! * `tags/loaders/legacy-fabric.svg` -- unknown attribute `clip-path` on <g>
//! * `tags/loaders/purpur.svg` -- non-uniform transform scale (1.1250 x 1.1372)
//! * `tags/loaders/quilt.svg` -- non-uniform transform scale (0.0305 x 0.0305)
//!
//! ## What is different from the iced file
//!
//! **A `PathShape` is one polyline.** iced's path builder holds subpaths and
//! cubic Beziers; `epaint::PathShape` holds a `Vec<Pos2>` and nothing else. So
//! the generator flattens every cubic at generation time -- recursive halving
//! until both control points are within 0.002 of the view box's long side
//! of the chord, which is 0.05 px at 24 px and 0.1 px at 48 px, well
//! under epaint's half-pixel feathering -- and splits every element into one
//! shape per subpath, because a `PathShape` cannot hold two. Flattening here
//! rather than per frame is the whole reason the coordinates in this file are
//! plain numbers: 23600 of them, and not one is computed twice.
//!
//! Splitting subpaths is exact for strokes: stroking a subpath does not
//! depend on its siblings, which is why the iced side's multi-subpath paths
//! and these shapes draw the same picture. It is **not** exact for fills, and
//! that is the one thing this file cannot say:
//!
//! **17 of the 33 filled elements have no egui
//! shape.** `PathShape` has a fill colour and no fill rule, and epaint
//! fills a path with a triangle fan from its first point. That fan covers a
//! contour exactly when the contour is star-shaped as seen from that point --
//! the toolkit documents convex, which is sufficient and not necessary -- so
//! an element of disjoint such contours is exact, one shape each. An element
//! with a **hole** or an **outline the fan cannot cover** is not: the hole is
//! filled solid (no fill rule to cut it, and a nested contour wound the other
//! way is subtracted by the reference's `nonzero`), or the fan paints ground
//! the outline does not. These:
//!
//! * `cloud.svg` -- element 1: a concave outline the fan cannot cover
//! * `cog.svg` -- element 1: a concave outline the fan cannot cover, `fill-rule="evenodd"`
//! * `online-indicator.svg` -- element 2: hole (a subpath nested inside another)
//! * `radio-button.svg` -- element 1: hole (a subpath nested inside another)
//! * `radio-button-checked.svg` -- element 1: hole (a subpath nested inside another)
//! * `spinner.svg` -- element 2: a concave outline the fan cannot cover
//! * `tags/categories/entities.svg` -- element 3: a concave outline the fan cannot cover, `fill-rule="evenodd"`
//! * `tags/categories/mobs.svg` -- element 3: a concave outline the fan cannot cover, `fill-rule="evenodd"`
//! * `tags/categories/potato.svg` -- element 13: hole (a subpath nested inside another)
//! * `tags/loaders/babric.svg` -- element 4: hole (a subpath nested inside another)
//! * `tags/loaders/bta-babric.svg` -- element 4: hole (a subpath nested inside another)
//! * `tags/loaders/datapack.svg` -- element 1: a concave outline the fan cannot cover, `fill-rule="evenodd"`
//! * `tags/loaders/minecraft.svg` -- element 1: a concave outline the fan cannot cover, `fill-rule="evenodd"`
//! * `tags/loaders/vanilla.svg` -- element 1: a concave outline the fan cannot cover, `fill-rule="evenodd"`
//! * `tags/loaders/velocity.svg` -- element 1: a concave outline the fan cannot cover; element 3: a concave outline the fan cannot cover
//! * `x.svg` -- element 1: a concave outline the fan cannot cover, `fill-rule="evenodd"`
//!
//! The iced side carries the reference's `fill-rule` and honours it, so those
//! glyphs are drawn correctly there and only approximately here. Nothing else
//! in the set is affected: the other 16 filled
//! elements are expressible.
//!
//! **10 elements are not here at all.** They are the shapes the
//! reference declares with neither a fill nor a stroke -- the clamp path every
//! exported icon carries, `M0 0h24v24H0Z` -- which SVG paints nothing for. The
//! iced file emits them as `Cmd::NoPaint` so a reader can see it read them; a
//! shape that paints nothing has no `PathShape` to be, so they are dropped and
//! named here instead:
//!
//! * `key.svg` element 1
//! * `loader.svg` element 1
//! * `palette.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/categories/entities.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/categories/mobs.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/loaders/bungeecord.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/loaders/fabric.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/loaders/forge.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/loaders/liteloader.svg` element 1
//! * `tags/loaders/paper.svg` element 1
//!
//! **The pen is not the same pen.** `PathStroke` carries a width, a colour and
//! a `StrokeKind`, and no line cap or join; epaint's stroker joins with miters
//! and has no round caps. The reference declares
//! `stroke-linecap="round"`/`stroke-linejoin="round"` on 413 of the 441
//! glyphs, so a corner here is slightly sharper than the reference's and a
//! line end is square rather than round. That is a property of the toolkit,
//! not of this file's data, and there is no attribute to set to change it.
//!
//! ## Performance
//!
//! These shapes are drawn every frame a window draws, so the cost model matters
//! more than the last decimal of a coordinate.
//!
//! * **The geometry is `&'static`.** No SVG is parsed at runtime, no path is
//!   built from a string, and the coordinates live in the binary. Drawing a
//!   glyph allocates one `Vec<Shape>` and one `Vec<Pos2>` per path, both
//!   pre-sized with `Vec::with_capacity`, and nothing else.
//! * **One paint-list entry per glyph, not one per path.** `draw` and `cached`
//!   hand egui a single `Shape::Vec`, so a 15-shape glyph costs one entry in
//!   the layer's paint list and one clip decision rather than fifteen, and the
//!   tessellator walks a contiguous batch.
//! * **What is *not* saved: tessellation.** egui caches tessellation for one
//!   frame and then throws it away -- there is no cross-frame mesh cache in the
//!   toolkit -- so a glyph that moves is tessellated again next frame. The cost
//!   this module removes is the allocation, the path building and the
//!   paint-list churn, not the triangles. `cached` goes one step further and
//!   keeps the built shapes across frames for a glyph that has not changed:
//!   a hit is a `Vec` clone, which is a memcpy of the points that were going to
//!   be computed anyway, and a miss is the same build `draw` does.
//!
//! A baked `Shape::Mesh` is the other route, and it is deliberately not taken.
//! A mesh is a finished triangle list, so adding one skips tessellation
//! entirely -- but egui bakes *absolute* positions into it and has no
//! per-shape transform at paint time, so a mesh cache has to include the
//! widget's position in its key. A scrolling list moves its icons every frame;
//! every one of them would miss, re-tessellate through a private `Tessellator`
//! per icon per frame, and the bounded cache would clear itself continuously.
//! That is strictly worse than handing shapes to the single tessellation pass
//! egui already runs at the end of the frame. Where icons do not move, the
//! shape route's cache hit is already a memcpy.

